{"id": 10, "ts": 1780695315.7995853, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "emily_brooks", "message": "I\u2019m inclined to move my provisional recommendation to **Candidate\u202fB**. While Candidate\u202fC\u2019s 100\u202f% reliability and rapid decision\u2011making are compelling, we have no concrete evidence of C\u2019s stress\u2011resilience\u2014a non\u2011negotiable criterion for long\u2011distance operations. In contrast, Candidate\u202fB is explicitly noted as \u201chandles stress very well,\u201d directly satisfying that critical requirement. B also scores high on operational reliability (\u201cvery conscientious\u201d) and brings strong technical aptitude (\u201cexcellent computer skills\u201d and good weather assessment). The concerns about B\u2019s tone and being \u201cpretentious\u201d are relevant, but they appear less safety\u2011critical than the lack of any stress\u2011resilience data for C. Given the importance of staying calm under pressure, I believe B offers the strongest overall fit at this stage.", "thoughts": "We have Sarah's answer: no info on C stress resilience.\n\nNow Emily must produce public message. She should consider that we lack stress resilience info for C, which is a key criterion. She may adjust recommendation or note uncertainty. She can compare with other candidates where stress resilience is known: B handles stress very well; D responds adequately; A unknown. So maybe B's stress handling is a strong point. She may shift recommendation to B or keep C but note missing info.\n\nGiven current discussion: Sarah Mitchell recommended C, but noted concerns. Emily can respond: I agree with C's reliability but note lack of stress resilience data, and B's known stress handling may make B a stronger overall candidate. She can propose shifting provisional recommendation to B, citing stress resilience and conscientiousness, while acknowledging B's communication tone issue.\n\nShe should reference explicit facts: B handles stress very well; B is very conscientious; B good at assessing weather; B excellent computer skills. Also concerns: pretentious, wrong tone. She can argue that stress resilience is critical for long-distance flights, and B's proven ability outweighs communication concerns which can be mitigated.\n\nThus Emily's vote: Candidate B.\n\nLet's craft message."}
